The MigrateLinkedChildren class is internal and its Perform method is public, but the class is internal so it is inaccessible outside its assembly. Also, the constructor requires several interfaces that are not found in the test project references. The JellyfinDbContext constructor requires parameters that are not trivial to mock or instantiate. The private method CleanupItemsFromDeletedLibraries is not accessible directly, and the logger used is internal to the class instance, so verifying the logger calls is difficult without refactoring.

Given these constraints, the best approach is to refactor the MigrateLinkedChildren class to make it more testable by extracting the logger and the DbContextFactory dependencies so they can be mocked and injected, and to make the class or the method virtual or public for testing. However, the apply_refactor tool is currently not available.

Since the user specifically wants coverage of the call on line 336 where _logger.LogInformation("No items from deleted libraries found."); is called, and this is inside the private method CleanupItemsFromDeletedLibraries, we can try to test the Perform method which calls this private method indirectly by setting up the DbContext to have no orphaned items from deleted libraries.

But since the class is internal, we cannot instantiate it directly in the test project. We can try to use InternalsVisibleTo attribute if it exists or is possible, but we cannot modify the source permanently.

Given these limitations, I will write a test class inside the same namespace Jellyfin.Server.Migrations.Routines and mark it as internal, so it can access internal types. I will also mock the dependencies and use Moq to verify the logger calls. I will test the Perform method and set up the DbContext mock to simulate the scenario where no orphaned items from deleted libraries exist, so the log message on line 336 is triggered.

I will submit this test class now.

